  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A widespread, soaking rainfall along with windy\nconditions are expected for Thanksgiving Day, with unsettled \nweather lasting into the weekend.\n\nNorthern and southern stream energy will phase with a large \nupper level low taking shape over the Tennessee Valley, as broad\nsurface low pressure tracks north into the Carolinas tonight \nwith a trough extending westward into the lower Ohio Valley. The\nsystem is then forecast to continue northward into the eastern \nGreat Lakes and Northeast later Thursday into Friday. Widespread\nprecipitation will spread northward into the region on Thursday\nas plume of deep moisture transport develops within an area of \nstrong deformation along the north flank of the upper low. \n\nDry conditions should begin Thanksgiving day as high pressure \nmaintains some influence, while continuing to slide to the east.\nRain should get into the southern Tier by late morning, then \nslowly spread northward through the remainder of the day into \nearly evening. The North Country may even remain rain free \nwell into the afternoon. Strong easterly jet aloft and tight \nsurface pressure gradient will bring windy conditions Thursday \nafternoon through Thursday night. Winds will likely remain just\nbelow below advisory criteria, gusting to 35 to 45 mph, with \nsome isolated gusts 45+ mph possible over the lakeshore of\nNiagara and Orleans counties, and farther south downwind of the\nChautauqua ridge.\n\nTemperatures aloft remain quite mild with profiles suggesting almost \nall rain as the primary precipitation type through Friday morning. \nA brief period of sleet is possible in higher elevations due to\nwet-bulbing, but this will quickly change to plain rain as\nprecip continues.\n\nFriday through Saturday, occasional showers will continue as the \ndeep mid level closed low crosses the region. The airmass will grow \ncold enough for some lake enhancement by Friday and Saturday, with\nindications pointing towards westerly flow during this time frame,\ngradually becoming southwesterly late Saturday into Saturday \nnight. Late Friday through Saturday, modest cold advection will \ndrop 850 MB temperatures down to -2C to -3C. Latest sounding \ndata and thickness levels suggest rain for lower elevations, but\nsome wet snow may start to mix in across higher terrain. Marginal\ntemperatures at the surface and aloft suggest there will be \nlittle, if any snow accumulation during this period, even across\nhigher terrain. Best chance for a light coating would be across\nthe higher terrain of the western Southern Tier.\n\nRainfall totals with this system remain in the 1.0\" to 1.5\" \nrange, but with much of the area in D1 moderate drought after \nthe exceptionally dry conditions since September, this is \nexpected to be a beneficial rainfall with minimal hydro concerns\nat this time.",
